R"PICTURE(Draw it as a pen-and-ink illustration for an e-ink notebook: confident black outlines on plain white paper, clear silhouettes, and only light, sparse hatching for form; no smooth gradients, airbrushing or dense texture. Prefer fewer, deliberate lines that stay legible when redrawn with a pen at notebook size. Keep one consistent viewpoint: every part appears as it would in that view, and in a cross-section cut structures show their cut profile. Color: when the subject naturally has colors (a red apple, a blue sky, green leaves, a yellow sun) or a standard textbook color convention exists (red arteries, blue veins), fill those parts with flat, clean, saturated color inside the black outlines, as a well-colored textbook figure would; keep diagrams and subjects without natural color in black ink, never add color for decoration or to make parts arbitrarily different, and leave the background white. No text, letters, numbers, labels, leader lines, arrows, borders or watermarks.)PICTURE"
